          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#LocallyCoveriantPerturbativeQuantumFieldTheory"} Locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#higher_categories_in_quantum_field_theory"} Higher categories in quantum field theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            %em Quantum field theory
            is the general framework for the description of the fundamental processes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            as understood today. These are carried by configurations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            under the generalized rules of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            is a quantum field theory and has been the main motivation for the development of the concept in general.
          %p
            Historically quantum field theory grew out of attempts to combine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+relativity"} special relativity
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                algebraic quantum field theory:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                – this encodes a quantum field theory as an assignment of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
                “of
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %li
              %p
                functorial quantum field theory:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
                – this encodes a quantum field theory as an assignment of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
                to patches of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
                1, and of maps between spaces of states – the time evolution operator – to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
                between such patches.
          %p
            Both these approaches aim at capturing the notion of a
            %em full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonperturbative+quantum+field+theory"} nonperturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            by its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            around some point. But since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonperturbative+quantum+field+theory"} nonperturbative quantum field theory
            has remained largely out of reach, and since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is already so rich, the latter is often understood by default when referring to the topic,.
          %p
            Recent times have seen major progress in understanding these axiomatizations and connecting them to the structures studied in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (see the references below), but still there is a large number of interesting phenomena in quantum field theory that physicists handle semi-rigorously and that are waiting for a fully formal understanding.
          %h2#LocallyCoveriantPerturbativeQuantumFieldTheory Locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            The formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            has many aspects and perspectives. Two almost complementary threads are the following:
          %p
            %strong
              1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
              by means of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
              expansions of (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"}> Wick rotated
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integrals"} path integrals
          %p
            This is the approach predominant in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
          %p
            It produces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numbers"} numbers
            which are checked to great precision in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            starting from the early development of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            and recently culminating in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
            physics seen at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            experiment, confirming the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            While many of the mathematical intricacies of this approach have found solutions over the decades, most of these rely on global properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            such as translation invariance and existence of an invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetimes"} quantum field theory on curved spacetimes
            of relevance in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole+radiation"} black hole radiation
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+in+QCD"} instanton vacuum of QCD
          %p
            %strong
              2)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
              by means of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets"} local nets
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
          %p
            This is the approach predominant in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
          %p
            It produces the structural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
            of quantum field theory, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PCT+theorem"} PCT theorem
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            and it seamlessly generalizes to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} QFT on curved spacetimes
          %p
            In its insistence on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            its ambition is to describe the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            quantum field theory. But as a matter of fact not a single relevant example (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
            QFT in spacetime dimensions 4 or greater) is known. Indeed, the construction of the motivating example,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p But more recently a synthesis of these two threads has been developed:
          %p
            %strong
              3)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
              by means of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets"} local nets
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
          %p
            This rests on the observation (
            %a{:href => "#IlinSlavnov78"}> Il’in-Slavnov 78
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaser73"}> Epstein-Glaser 73
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Scharf95"} Scharf 95
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
          %p
            This formulation hence allows to construct the usual examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbative quantum field theories
            in a rigorous fashion, and then to extend them to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetimes"} quantum field theory on curved spacetimes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagenRejzner13"}> Brunetti-Fredenhagen-Rejzner 13
            ).
          %p There remains just one problem:
          %p
            When applied to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetime"} on curved spacetime
            the usual axioms on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            break: either they enforce all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+classes"} characteristic classes
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sectors"}> instanton sectors
            ”) to be trivial, or else the axioms encoding locality are broken (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schenkel14"} Schenkel 14
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Higher+field+bundles+for+gauge+fields"} Schreiber 14
          %p
            This remaining problem is solved by passing from plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebras
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"}> higher algebras
            ), and hence to a formulation of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} homotopical algebraic quantum field theory
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Schenkel17"} Schenkel 17
          %h2#higher_categories_in_quantum_field_theory Higher categories in quantum field theory
          %p
            (See also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"} higher category theory and physics
            and (
            %a{:href => "#SatiSchreiber"}> SatiSchreiber
            )).
          %p
            Even though
            %em quantum field theory
            has been around for decades and has been very successful both as a phenomenological model in experimental physics as well as a source of deep mathematical structures and theorems, from a mathematical perspective it is still to a large extent mysterious, though recently much progress is being made.
          %p There are essentially two alternative approaches for formalizing quantum field theory and making it accessible to mathematical treatment:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em> algebraic quantum field theory
                \:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %li
              %p
                %em> functorial quantum field theory
                \:
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %p
            (Other structures which are used to define quantum field theories, such as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
          %p
            Both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            involve the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Roberts"} John Roberts
                first conceived the idea of
                = succeed "-categorical" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
                in the context of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %li
              %p
                the description of 2-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
                and 3-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TFT"} TFT
                in terms of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+categories"} modular tensor categories
                provides a major application of the theory of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            %li
              %p
                the notion of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
                was motivated from
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %li
              %p
                In early 1990s
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+categories"} A-∞ categories
                first appeared in works of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
                and Fukaya on the categorical description of twisted
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
                was formulated by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Dolan"} Jim Dolan
                in the context of extended
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %li
              %p
                various structures involving
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operad"} (∞,1)-operad
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+chiral+homology"} topological chiral homology
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blob+homology"} blob homology
                are motivated by, and find their application in, the algebraic description of quantum field theory;
            %li
              %p
                the description of higher background
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
                is intrinsically about
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
                theory.
          %p
            There are some indications that such
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical structures
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
          %p Ours is the age to figure this out.
